    },
    "body": "Some descriptor error messages quote the string the caller sent, and a descriptor can hold a private key (a WIF, an xprv). The reply then carries the key into places the request may not go: an application's error log, an error reporter, a screenshot shared for help. Even the first characters of a key give away part of it.\n\nThis PR stops the quoting.\n\n**Parsing** (`descriptor.cpp`, `util/bip32.cpp`):\n\n| before | after |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Key ' <key>' is invalid due to whitespace` | `Key is invalid due to whitespace` |\n| `key '<key>' is not valid` | `key is not valid` |\n| `Pubkey '<hex>' is invalid` | `Pubkey is invalid` |\n| `Multi threshold '<text>' is not valid` | same for text of 11 characters or fewer, the length of `4294967295h`; `Multi threshold is not valid` for longer text |\n| `Key path value '<text>' specifies multipath in a section where multipath is not allowed` | same rule: `Key path value specifies multipath in a section where multipath is not allowed` for longer text |\n| `Key path value '<text>' is not a valid uint32` | same rule: `Key path value is not a valid uint32` for longer text |\n| `'<expr>' is not a valid descriptor function` | `'<name>(...' is not a valid descriptor function`, where `<name>` is the text up to `(`; `Not a valid descriptor function` if there is no `(` or the name is longer than 16 characters |\n\nNo key is 11 characters or fewer.\n\n**Callers**:\n\n- `Cannot derive script without private keys: '...'` (`rpc/util.cpp`) and `Could not add descriptor '...': ...` (`wallet/imports.cpp`) now quote `desc->ToString()`, the public form with its checksum.\n- `Unable to parse descriptor '...': ...` (`wallet/rpc/spend.cpp`) no longer quotes the string: there is no parsed descriptor to print, and the parse error says what is wrong.\n\nExisting assertions on these messages are updated. New tests check that no part of a key is in the parse errors and the caller errors, including a key with a typo in its last character. They also check the exact text of the caller errors.\n\nThis is one of several PRs from a large audit of Bitcoin Core that I did with the help of LLM tools. The audit produced its evidence all at once, which is why the PRs arrive together. I understand each change fully and will answer review questions myself.\n",
